      "text": "To find the volume of the frustum, we first need to determine the volume of the original pyramid and the volume of the smaller pyramid that was removed.\n\nThe volume \\( V \\) of a pyramid is given by the formula:\n\\[ V = \\frac{1}{3} \\times \\text{Base Area} \\times \\text{Height} \\]\n\nFirst, we calculate the volume of the original pyramid. The base is a square with side length 12 cm, so the base area is:\n\\[ \\text{Base Area} = 12 \\times 12 = 144 \\text{ cm}^2 \\]\nThe height of the original pyramid is 8 cm. Therefore, the volume of the original pyramid is:\n\\[ V_{\\text{original}} = \\frac{1}{3} \\times 144 \\times 8 = \\frac{1}{3} \\times 1152 = 384 \\text{ cm}^3 \\]\n\nNext, we calculate the volume of the smaller pyramid. The base is a square with side length 6 cm, so the base area is:\n\\[ \\text{Base Area} = 6 \\times 6 = 36 \\text{ cm}^2 \\]\nThe height of the smaller pyramid is 4 cm. Therefore, the volume of the smaller pyramid is:\n\\[ V_{\\text{smaller}} = \\frac{1}{3} \\times 36 \\times 4 = \\frac{1}{3} \\times 144 = 48 \\text{ cm}^3 \\]\n\nThe volume of the frustum is the volume of the original pyramid minus the volume of the smaller pyramid:\n\\[ V_{\\text{frustum}} = V_{\\text{original}} - V_{\\text{smaller}} = 384 - 48 = 336 \\text{ cm}^3 \\]\n\nThus, the volume of the frustum is:\n\\[ \\boxed{336} \\]",
